<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/indiana-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal Clause Representation</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Appraisal Clause Representation for Indiana Policyholders</h1><p class="lede">Your carrier has accepted the loss but the number is stuck. The appraisal clause in your policy replaces the back-and-forth with a three-person panel, and we sit in the chair you appoint.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Your role for us</dt><dd>Party appraiser</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Settles</dt><dd>Amount of loss only</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>The paragraph most Indiana policyholders never read</h2>
<p>Look near the back of almost any Indiana homeowners, farm or commercial property form and you will find a short section headed “Appraisal.” The wording varies by carrier, but the bones are consistent: if you and the company disagree on the amount of loss, either side can demand appraisal in writing. Each side picks a competent and impartial appraiser, those two pick an umpire, and a written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>Indiana has no separate statute that writes this process for you. Your rights come from the policy itself, which is why the first thing we do on any file is read your actual form and endorsements rather than a summary.</p>
<h2>What appraisal clause representation includes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deadline check.</strong> Many forms give each side 20 days after the demand to name an appraiser. We confirm the clock and any carve-outs before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>Our own inspection.</strong> We walk, measure and photograph the property ourselves, so our scope does not inherit the gaps in the carrier’s.</li>
<li><strong>A line-item valuation</strong> priced for your Indiana ZIP code with current material and labor figures, code-required items and overhead and profit addressed the way your policy reads.</li>
<li><strong>Panel work.</strong> We meet the carrier’s appraiser, trade line-by-line positions and narrow the dispute to the items that genuinely differ.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation.</strong> If items remain, we help choose a qualified umpire and present photos, measurements and pricing support for each one.</li>
<li><strong>Award review.</strong> Before anyone signs, we make sure replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value are each stated clearly on the award form.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Know the limits</b>Appraisal decides how much a covered loss costs. It does not decide coverage, and it does not award damages for bad faith. Indiana courts recognize a duty of good faith and fair dealing between insurers and policyholders, but whether a carrier breached it is a legal question for an Indiana attorney, not an appraisal panel.</div>
<h2>Files where appraisal tends to work</h2>
<p>Scope and price disputes are where appraisal earns its fee: how many slopes the hail actually bruised, whether discontinued siding can be matched, what drywall, insulation and paint cost in Hamilton County this season. It is a poor fit when the real argument is an exclusion, a late-notice defense or wear and tear.</p>
<h2>Why we price what we can defend</h2>
<p>An umpire sees inflated numbers every week and discounts them. Our estimates are built to be accepted line by line, which is what actually moves an award. If we think the panel will land closer to the carrier on an item, we tell you before you spend money arguing it.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can my roofer serve as my appraiser?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most Indiana policies require each appraiser to be competent and impartial or disinterested. A contractor who stands to profit from the repair can invite a challenge to the award, so an independent appraiser is usually the safer choice.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the insurer will not name its appraiser?</summary><div class="a"><p>Policies typically set a window for each side to name an appraiser. If the carrier misses it or refuses to participate, the options depend on your policy wording and Indiana law, and that is the point to involve an attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does an Indiana appraisal take?</summary><div class="a"><p>Straightforward residential files often wrap up within two to three months of both appraisers being named. Large commercial losses, contents-heavy claims and umpire hearings take longer.</p></div></details></div>
